<interrogationes unitas="arbor2_glr">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I parse a whole C source file into an AST?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexator_creare arbor2_lexema_proximum arbor2_token_ex_lexema arbor2_expansion_creare arbor2_glr_creare arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit" via="probationes/probatio_arbor2_file_roundtrip.c">Lex
      the text with arbor2_lexator_creare, wrap every lexeme from
      arbor2_lexema_proximum with arbor2_token_ex_lexema into a Xar of
      Arbor2Token* (stop after the EOF lexeme), make the parser with
      arbor2_glr_creare(piscina, intern, expansion) using an
      Arbor2Expansion from arbor2_expansion_creare, and call
      arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit. The radix is a
      TRANSLATION_UNIT node; datum.translation_unit.declarationes holds
      one node per top-level item (declaration, function, directive,
      ERROR), plus any floating comments it promoted (appended after
      the item they stand in front of). Lexing directly
      does no macro expansion, which is what a byte-exact roundtrip
      wants: the parser consults the expansion only for typedef and
      macro lookahead, #if/#ifdef evaluation and recording the typedefs
      it parses. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): inside a function
      body every GLR fork appends in place to one shared CORPUS
      sententiae Xar, so an ambiguous statement such as foo * bar; with
      foo unknown is stored once per surviving interpretation, and the
      statements after it are duplicated too.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a parsed tree back into the exact source text?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_scribere arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit" via="probationes/probatio_arbor2_file_roundtrip.c">Call
      arbor2_scribere(piscina, radix). It walks the tree and emits every
      kept token with its spatia_ante and spatia_post (whitespace and
      comments), returning a chorda* that is NOT NUL-terminated; the
      roundtrip suite compares it with the input for the fixtures it
      lists (color.c, utf8.c and base64.c are commented out). ERROR
      nodes are skipped, so the tokens of an item that failed to parse
      are missing from the output; an AMBIGUUS node prints its first
      interpretation; a CONDITIONALIS prints the raw tokens of its
      branches, not their parsed trees. A COMMENTUM node that the
      parser promoted into declarationes (a floating comment) is
      written as well, although the same comment is still in the next
      token's spatia_ante, so such a comment comes out twice. Caution
      (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): an ambiguous statement inside a
      function body (foo * bar; with foo unknown) is stored once per
      surviving fork in the shared CORPUS, so arbor2_scribere prints it,
      and the statements after it, more than once and the output is not
      byte-identical to the input.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tell whether a translation-unit parse hit syntax errors?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit arbor2_glr_exspectata_pro_statu">Not
      from successus: it stays VERUM when items failed. Each failed item
      becomes an ERROR node (datum.error.lexemata_saltata = its tokens up
      to and including the ';' or '}' that ends it, datum.error.nuntius =
      a chorda* such as 'Inexpectatum X, exspectata: ...' built from
      arbor2_glr_exspectata_pro_statu, or NIHIL when no message was
      recorded) and parsing resumes at the next item. res.errores is
      NIHIL when the failure count is zero, otherwise the Xar of
      chorda* messages. Only when more than ARBOR2_GLR_MAX_ERRORES (X)
      items have failed does it give up with successus FALSUM and the
      partial TRANSLATION_UNIT. Caveats in the current code: every
      ERROR node's nuntius is element 0 of the shared message list, so
      from the second failure on it is normally the first failure's
      message, not its own; and each non-empty branch of an #if, #ifdef or
      #ifndef block is parsed by a nested call that clears that list
      and the failure count, so a failure before the block can leave
      res.errores NIHIL. The reliable check is to walk declarationes,
      and the parsed TRANSLATION_UNIT of every conditional branch, for
      ERROR nodes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is an AMBIGUUS node and where do I find them after a parse?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio">When
      two fork paths reach the same state after a shift with values
      that compare unequal, the parser keeps both. When several paths
      accept at the end they are judged first: a bare IDENTIFICATOR loses
      to the other value, between two TERNARIUS the one with a ternary
      in its falsum wins, and only otherwise both are kept.
      datum.ambiguus.interpretationes is a Xar of Arbor2Nodus* with two
      entries, a third interpretation nests another AMBIGUUS inside, and
      datum.ambiguus.identificator is a chorda* copy of the last token
      shifted when they met (NIHIL if that token was not an identifier).
      The comparison is shallow: only the node genus, plus the three
      children of a TERNARIUS, so two different trees with the same top
      genus are merged silently and the first is kept. The classic case
      is foo * bar with foo unknown: multiplication or pointer
      declaration. datum.ambiguus.genus is always
      ARBOR2_AMBIG_TYPEDEF_IGNOTUM today. The translation-unit result's
      ambigui is NIHIL (except on the give-up path), so walk the tree or
      read glr->ambigui, which collects every AMBIGUUS that parser ever
      made, including ones on fork paths that later died. Caution (audit
      2026-10-18, unfixed): inside a function body the forks also append
      to one shared CORPUS sententiae Xar in place, so foo * bar; there
      is stored once per surviving interpretation and the statements
      after it are duplicated too.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make MyType * p; parse as a declaration instead of an ambiguity?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_addere_typedef arbor2_expansion_addere_macro arbor2_glr_est_probabiliter_typus">Tell
      the expansion before parsing: arbor2_expansion_addere_typedef(exp,
      "MyType") takes a NUL-terminated name; for a macro type such as i32
      use arbor2_expansion_addere_macro with a body that begins with a
      type keyword. When a state offers several actions and the token on
      the top of the stack satisfies arbor2_glr_est_probabiliter_typus (a
      registered typedef, or a macro whose body, followed through one
      level of macro-to-macro expansion, starts with int, char, void,
      float, double, long, short, signed, unsigned, struct, union, enum,
      typedef, const or volatile), only the SHIFT actions are kept (the
      REDUCE that starts the expression path is dropped).
      arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit also registers each top-level
      typedef it parses into the same expansion, so later items in the
      file see it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I reuse one Arbor2GLR or Arbor2Expansion for several parses?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_creare arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit arbor2_expansion_addere_typedef" via="probationes/probatio_arbor2_file_roundtrip.c">Yes,
      but state carries over. arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio never clears
      glr->errores, glr->ambigui or the counters num_furcae, num_mergae
      and max_frons, so a later result's errores still lists earlier
      failures; only the translation-unit parser clears errores and
      num_errores, and nothing ever clears glr->ambigui. There is no
      reset function. Typedefs learned from one file stay registered
      in the Arbor2Expansion (the roundtrip suite shares one expansion
      across all its fixtures). Nothing is freed: each table lookup
      allocates a new Xar and the GSS punctum_salutis marks are never
      rolled back, so memory grows with the input until the piscina
      goes. A fresh parser and expansion per file is the safe
      pattern.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between arbor2_glr_parsere, arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio and arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit">arbor2_glr_parsere
      only calls arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio, and despite its name that
      runs the whole table from state 0: one expression, statement,
      declaration or function definition, accepted at EOF (an EOF token
      is optional; running off the end of the Xar counts as EOF). It does
      no error recovery (successus FALSUM, radix NIHIL) and knows nothing
      of # directives. arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit splits the
      tokens into top-level items at ';' and '}' boundaries, parses each
      with arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio (the ';' of a declaration is left
      out, except for typedefs, and set afterwards as tok_semicolon), and
      adds directive nodes, ERROR recovery, typedef registration and
      comment promotion.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how are #ifdef, #if and #define represented in the tree?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit arbor2_conditio_evaluare arbor2_conditio_est_definitum">A
      conditional block becomes ARBOR2_NODUS_CONDITIONALIS;
      datum.conditionalis.rami is a Xar of Arbor2CondRamus*, one per
      #if/#ifdef/#ifndef, #elif and #else, and endif_lexemata keeps the
      #endif line. Every branch is kept: lexemata holds its tokens, parsed
      its own TRANSLATION_UNIT (NIHIL for an empty branch; nested blocks
      appear inside it), directivum_lexemata its directive line for the
      roundtrip, and valor_evaluatus with est_evaluatum the condition
      judged against the expansion's macros. #define, #undef, #pragma and
      #include become DEFINE, UNDEF, PRAGMA and INCLUDE nodes holding the
      raw line in lexemata_originalia; the INCLUDE info is NIHIL because
      nothing is resolved. Limits in the current code: a block with no
      #endif loses its last branch (tokens and all) without an error,
      and an #if or #elif expression continued with a backslash is
      evaluated only up to the backslash.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does #ifdef X evaluate false although the file #defines X just above?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_conditio_est_definitum arbor2_expansion_addere_macro" via="lib/arbor2_glr.worklog.md">The
      parser is not a preprocessor: per the worklog, directive tokens are
      stored verbatim for a perfect roundtrip and all branches are kept,
      unlike a preprocessor that picks one. A DEFINE node only notes
      nomen_macro and est_functio; nothing is added to the
      Arbor2Expansion. #ifdef and #ifndef are judged with
      arbor2_conditio_est_definitum, #if and #elif with
      arbor2_conditio_evaluare, against macros already in the expansion
      (for example from arbor2_expansion_addere_macro). The value is
      informational only: every branch is parsed either way.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I have preprocessor directives inside a function body?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit" via="lib/arbor2_glr_tabula.c lib/arbor2_glr.worklog.md">No.
      Directives are recognised only where a top-level item (or an item
      of a conditional branch) starts; the worklog records that they are
      handled at translation-unit level, not in the grammar tables, and
      ARBOR2_LEXEMA_HASH does not occur in lib/arbor2_glr_tabula.c. So an
      #ifdef or #define between braces makes the whole enclosing function
      fail and come back as one ERROR node. Top-level directives other
      than the conditionals, #define, #undef, #pragma and #include (for
      example #error or #line) fail the same way, and that ERROR runs to
      the next top-level ';' or the '}' closing the next brace block, so
      it can swallow the following declaration or function too.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a brace initializer be followed by more declarators, as in int a[2] = {1, 2}, b; ?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere_translation_unit arbor2_glr_parsere" via="lib/arbor2_glr.c probationes/probatio_arbor2_glr.c">Not
      at top level today. The item boundary finder ends an item at a '}'
      that returns brace depth to zero unless the next token is ';' or
      an identifier or '*' with no '(' in the next five tokens. A ','
      after the initializer therefore cuts the item after '}', and the
      rest (, b;) becomes an ERROR node. The grammar itself accepts the
      form: probationes/probatio_arbor2_glr.c parses int a[] = {1}, b[]
      = {2} with arbor2_glr_parsere. In a whole file, int a[2] = {1,
      2}; alone is fine; write the declarators as separate
      declarations.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I print an identifier from a node with %s?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_nodus_genus_nomen arbor2_nt_nomen arbor2_actio_genus_nomen" via="tools/glr_debug.c">No:
      every name in the tree is a chorda - datum.folium.valor,
      datum.membrum.membrum, datum.declarator.titulus,
      *datum.ambiguus.identificator - holding the lexer's interned copy
      of the token text, with datum and mensura but no terminating NUL.
      Print it with "%.*s", (integer)c.mensura, c.datum, the %.*s form
      tools/glr_debug.c uses. The helpers arbor2_nodus_genus_nomen,
      arbor2_nt_nomen and arbor2_actio_genus_nomen do return C strings;
      they answer "IGNOTUM" for values they do not list (INCLUDE, DEFINE,
      UNDEF and PRAGMA among the node genera, and fifteen non-terminals
      such as ARBOR2_NT_INIT_DECLARATOR).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does parsing print thousands of [DEBUG] State lines to stdout?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_glr_parsere_expressio" via="lib/arbor2_glr.c">lib/arbor2_glr.c
      defines GLR_DEBUG as 1 at its top, without an #ifndef guard, so
      every action lookup, SHIFT, REDUCE and GOTO is printed with printf
      to stdout. There is no runtime switch, and -DGLR_DEBUG=0 would be a
      macro redefinition (an error under -Werror): set the define to 0 in
      the source to silence it, or keep output that matters off stdout.
      GLR_TRACE_SLOWDOWN, 0 by default, adds per-item timing lines when
      turned on.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
